      "text": "To find the minimum value of the function \\( z = x^2 + 2y^2 + 6x - 4y + 22 \\), we can complete the square for both the \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) terms.\n\nFirst, consider the \\(x\\) terms:\n\\[ x^2 + 6x. \\]\nTo complete the square, we add and subtract \\(9\\):\n\\[ x^2 + 6x = (x+3)^2 - 9. \\]\n\nNext, consider the \\(y\\) terms:\n\\[ 2y^2 - 4y. \\]\nFactor out the 2:\n\\[ 2y^2 - 4y = 2(y^2 - 2y) = 2((y-1)^2 - 1) = 2(y-1)^2 - 2. \\]\n\nNow substitute these completed squares back into the original function:\n\\[ z = (x+3)^2 - 9 + 2(y-1)^2 - 2 + 22. \\]\nCombine the constant terms:\n\\[ z = (x+3)^2 + 2(y-1)^2 + 11. \\]\n\nThe expression \\((x+3)^2 + 2(y-1)^2\\) is a sum of squares, which is always non-negative and achieves its minimum value of \\(0\\) when both squares are zero. This occurs when \\(x + 3 = 0\\) and \\(y - 1 = 0\\), i.e., when \\(x = -3\\) and \\(y = 1\\).\n\nSubstitute \\(x = -3\\) and \\(y = 1\\) into the function to find the minimum value of \\(z\\):\n\\[ z = 0 + 2(0) + 11 = 11. \\]\n\nThus, the minimum value of \\(z\\) is \\(\\boxed{11}\\).",
